-- js-modules.rofl — the CLAIMS the module-graph model makes about itself.
--
-- Rules live in rules/js-modules.rofl; this file is the ledger side: which
-- layer, which cells are handled, which are deliberately out and why, which
-- vocabulary the shape rules read, and what the model was CHECKED against.
--
-- Nothing here is asserted about the domain. Every row is a statement about
-- the MODEL, and every row is meant to be contradictable by a rule in
-- rules/js-modules.rofl or rules/js-model.rofl.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE LAYER. One fact. That is the whole claim of docs/modelling-a-language.md
-- and it is exercised here rather than argued: adding this line makes the
-- coverage audit enumerate a module-graph cell for every node kind that
-- exists, with no edit to any rule anywhere.
layer(modules).

-- The node kinds this layer actually walks, beyond the two already in
-- facts/js-kinds.rofl (import_declaration, import_expression). A kind claimed
-- but absent from node_kind evaporates silently — rules/js-model.rofl's
-- orphan_claim exists because that happened — so a claim about a specifier
-- kind needs the kind to be in the vocabulary first.
node_kind(js, import_specifier).
node_kind(js, import_default_specifier).
node_kind(js, import_namespace_specifier).

-- ...and the two kinds that carry a module specifier and are NOT imports.
-- Named so the audit can see them, ignored so the silence is not an option.
node_kind(js, export_named_declaration).
node_kind(js, export_all_declaration).
-- ...and the THIRD export form, declared 2026-09-06. It carries no specifier,
-- so it was outside the sentence above and outside this pack's whole subject —
-- and it was emitted by the scanner with no cell anywhere, which is exactly what
-- `vocabulary_gap[audit]` reported the moment a default export entered the
-- corpus. It is here rather than in the control-flow pack that needed it,
-- because a kind belongs with its family and not with its first consumer.
node_kind(js, export_default_declaration).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHAT THE HOST SUPPLIES, DECLARED (docs/modelling-a-language.md, rule 2).
--
-- A dirty emitter is allowed if the contamination is DECLARED and therefore
-- countable. These two are, and the first one is a statement about the KERNEL
-- rather than about JavaScript: path resolution is string surgery, and when
-- this row was written no rule could take a string apart at all.
--
-- **CORRECTED 2026-09-04: THE RETIREMENT CONDITION BELOW IS MET AND THIS ROW
-- STILL STANDS.** `src/reflect.ts` carries `str_char/2`, `str_len/1`,
-- `str_pre/2`, `str_seg/3` and `str_segs/2`, all reachable through `is`, so
-- three of the four relations declared here are now spellable in a rule
-- (`str_char0` is `str_char` at 0; `str_scheme` is `str_pre` on a colon) and
-- the sentence this comment used to make about the builtin vocabulary was
-- false. Whether to retire the loan is a DECISION and not a repair — the
-- resolver moved to the host as an OBSERVATION, so re-deriving these in rules
-- re-arms a second model nobody has decided to keep — and it is recorded as
-- `f_the_str_algebra_loan_reached_its_retirement_condition_unnoticed`. What is
-- not in doubt is that the comment may not go on asserting the old vocabulary.
--
-- Note what this says about the audit: nothing goes red when a loan becomes
-- RETIRABLE, only when one is undeclared. The shape layer has exactly this
-- detector — `shape_stale[audit]` — and `host_emitter` has none.
--
-- Neither emitter carries import semantics: `str_*` would split a shopping
-- list the same way, and `fs_*` reports what is on disk. What they are NOT
-- allowed to do is decide anything, and the count of these rows going to zero
-- is what "the kernel grew a string primitive" would mean, measurably.
host_emitter(js, modules, str_algebra, a_kernel_has_no_string_builtins).
host_emitter(js, modules, fs_listing,  a_no_ast_can_see_the_disk).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE BARE-FORM BUILTIN VOCABULARY, with the canonical spelling beside it.
--
-- `node:fs` is classified by its SCHEME and needs no list. This table is only
-- for the un-prefixed form, `import fs from 'fs'`, and it carries the `node:`
-- spelling as DATA because prefixing a string is not something a rule can do
-- with the kernel's builtins — the canonical name is what makes a builtin
-- compare exactly against node's own resolver instead of approximately.
--
-- Measured over this repository: 371 builtin imports, every single one
-- `node:`-prefixed, so this table matches nothing here. It exists because
-- babel's grammar and node's resolver both still accept the bare form, and a
-- fixture exercises it so the branch is not a rule nothing runs.
--
-- Keyed on the WHOLE specifier, not on its first segment: `fs/promises` is
-- deliberately absent, falls through to `bare`, and shows up as an
-- under-report against the oracle rather than as a rule guessing.
node_builtin_bare("assert",         "node:assert").
node_builtin_bare("buffer",         "node:buffer").
node_builtin_bare("child_process",  "node:child_process").
node_builtin_bare("crypto",         "node:crypto").
node_builtin_bare("events",         "node:events").
node_builtin_bare("fs",             "node:fs").
node_builtin_bare("http",           "node:http").
node_builtin_bare("https",          "node:https").
node_builtin_bare("module",         "node:module").
node_builtin_bare("os",             "node:os").
node_builtin_bare("path",           "node:path").
node_builtin_bare("process",        "node:process").
node_builtin_bare("readline",       "node:readline").
node_builtin_bare("stream",         "node:stream").
node_builtin_bare("string_decoder", "node:string_decoder").
node_builtin_bare("test",           "node:test").
node_builtin_bare("timers",         "node:timers").
node_builtin_bare("tty",            "node:tty").
node_builtin_bare("url",            "node:url").
node_builtin_bare("util",           "node:util").
node_builtin_bare("worker_threads", "node:worker_threads").
node_builtin_bare("zlib",           "node:zlib").

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE VERDICT PER SHAPE. Total over the shapes the rules can produce, and
-- the audit reports a shape that occurs without one.
--
-- The reason taxonomy is fixed at four: `runtime_dependent` is irreducible and
-- the other three are ours. Exactly one cell in this whole construct is
-- irreducible, and it is the dynamic import with a computed argument.
shape_verdict(relative,     resolves).
shape_verdict(node_builtin, resolves).
shape_verdict(bare,         out_of_scope).
shape_verdict(subpath,      not_yet).
shape_verdict(computed,     runtime_dependent).

-- `budget_exhausted` is the fourth reason and is deliberately NOT claimed:
-- nothing here ran out of budget, and rules/js-modules.rofl's
-- reason_unexercised would report it as decoration if it were written down.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE FRONTIER, per (kind, reason). The audit checks BOTH directions: a
-- reason missing for something that happens, and a reason recorded for
-- something that never does.
--
-- THE REFINEMENT, ADDED 2026-09-04, AND WHY THE COARSE ROWS BELOW ARE GONE.
-- These three were written as facts about a KIND, and all three were true at
-- once while the same kind was also `handled` — because the rule really does
-- resolve a relative specifier while a bare one is out of scope and a subpath
-- one is not done. A matrix keyed by kind reads that as an excuse outliving its
-- cause: `stale_reason[audit]` fired three FALSE rows in the only world that
-- loads every fact pack, and no test built that world. The cell was PARTIAL,
-- which is the member_expression limit arriving in a second layer.
--
-- The refinement is the SPECIFIER, and it is not invented here: rules/
-- js-modules.rofl already derives `src_shape` over exactly these four forms.
-- The values ride the shared refinement column and `shape_in` keeps them out of
-- the call graph, where a specifier means nothing.
axis_applies(shape, modules).

-- THE VALUES ARE THE ONES THE MODEL ALREADY DERIVES. `src_shape` in
-- rules/js-modules.rofl classifies every specifier as relative, node_builtin,
-- bare or subpath, and `shape_verdict` below already maps each to a verdict.
-- The first draft of this block invented a parallel `sp_*` vocabulary and was
-- wrong for the reason this repository keeps paying for: a second name for a
-- thing that already has one is a table that can disagree with itself.
shape_of(js, import_declaration, relative).
shape_of(js, import_declaration, node_builtin).
shape_of(js, import_declaration, bare).
shape_of(js, import_declaration, subpath).
shape_of(js, import_expression,  relative).
shape_of(js, import_expression,  node_builtin).
-- NOT bare and NOT subpath for a dynamic import: `reason_unexercised[audit]`
-- said no when both were declared, because this corpus contains neither and a
-- reason recorded for something that never happens is decoration.
-- only a dynamic import can have a specifier that is not text at all
shape_of(js, import_expression,  computed).

shape_in(relative,     modules).
shape_in(node_builtin, modules).
shape_in(bare,         modules).
shape_in(subpath,      modules).
shape_in(computed,     modules).

-- out_of_scope: a bare specifier is a package name and resolving it means
-- walking node_modules, reading package.json "exports", and following the
-- conditions map. 1.5% of this repository's imports; deliberately out.
unknown_because(js, import_declaration, bare, modules, out_of_scope).

-- not_yet: `#foo` needs the importing package's package.json "imports" map.
-- Zero occurrences in this repository and it still needs a verdict, because
-- the denominator is babel's grammar rather than the corpus.
unknown_because(js, import_declaration, subpath, modules, not_yet).

-- runtime_dependent: THE irreducible cell, and now it is a cell rather than a
-- kind. `import(pathVar)` names a module that does not exist until the
-- expression is evaluated; `import("./x.js")` is an ordinary edge, and before
-- the refinement those two shared one row.
unknown_because(js, import_expression, computed, modules, runtime_dependent).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE CELLS, CLAIMED.
handled(js, import_declaration, relative,     modules, r_import_decl_module_edge).
handled(js, import_declaration, node_builtin, modules, r_import_decl_module_edge).
handled(js, import_expression,  relative,     modules, r_dynamic_import_literal).
handled(js, import_expression,  node_builtin, modules, r_dynamic_import_literal).
handled(js, import_specifier,            modules, r_named_binding).
handled(js, import_default_specifier,    modules, r_default_binding).
handled(js, import_namespace_specifier,  modules, r_namespace_binding).

-- ...AND THE CELLS DELIBERATELY OUT.
--
-- `export { q } from './d'` and `export * from './d'` are module-graph edges
-- by every definition — a re-export both DEPENDS and FLOWS — and they are not
-- imports. They are named here so that the gap is a row rather than a silence;
-- the work is one more rule over the same `source` child and it is not done.
-- RECLASSIFIED 2026-09-05 BY THE SWEEP, and the correction is about the CLASS
-- of the verdict rather than about the cell. These two were `ignored` - a
-- waiver, which says the language offers nothing here to model - while the
-- comment directly above them said "module-graph edges by every definition"
-- and "the work is one more rule over the same `source` child and it is not
-- done". That is `not_yet`: open work counted as settled, which flatters the
-- coverage number by exactly two cells and hides them from `open_cell`.
--
-- MEASURED before changing it, on a probe holding both forms: `export { q }
-- from './y'` and `export * from './z'` produce ZERO import sites while three
-- ordinary imports in the same file produce three. The nodes are in the tree
-- and the layer does not look at them.
--
-- THE TELL GENERALISES: a waiver whose own comment describes undone work is a
-- waiver in the wrong class, and no audit can see it - `bad_reason` checks the
-- atom is in the vocabulary and both atoms were, `stale_reason` checks a reason
-- outliving its cause and this one had not. Only reading the sentence beside it
-- does, which is why a sweep reads them.
unknown_because(js, export_named_declaration, modules, not_yet).
unknown_because(js, export_all_declaration,   modules, not_yet).
-- ...and the default form is open for the SAME reason and owned by the same
-- item: this layer has one edge kind, and `export default` is not it.
unknown_because(js, export_default_declaration, modules, not_yet).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE EVIDENCE. Not a claim: the number of fixture import sites whose
-- resolution was compared, SET FOR SET, against node's own resolver
-- (import.meta.resolve, cross-checked with createRequire().resolve) —
-- an oracle that reads no ROFL fact and enumerates its own sites from babel.
--
--   checked(Lang, Kind, Layer, Rule, Oracle, Fixtures)
checked(js, import_declaration, modules, r_import_decl_module_edge, o_node_resolver, 15).
checked(js, import_expression,  modules, r_dynamic_import_literal,  o_node_resolver, 2).


-- ===========================================================================
-- THE MODULES SWEEP, 2026-09-05, and it is the last bucket. Thirty-nine cells,
-- and the pattern of the previous three held for a fourth time: measuring first
-- changed the answer for five of them.
--
--   2  ALREADY MODELLED AND NEVER RECORDED. `site_source` reads
--      `ast_node(Src, string_literal, _, _), ast_value(Src, S)` - the specifier
--      TEXT is the whole input to resolution - and the three binding rules read
--      `ast_name` off an identifier. Both kinds are load-bearing here and both
--      cells said nothing. Fourth and fifth instance of the matrix
--      under-reporting its own coverage.
--   1  unreachable under this parser: `import` is babel's `Import` node.
--   2  went to an item, plus a third that was already open.
--  34  residue, below.
--
-- AND TWO CELLS THAT WERE NOT IN THE BUCKET AT ALL, corrected here because a
-- sweep that walks past a false verdict in its own layer is the silence this
-- repository forbids. See the reclassification below.
ignored(js, array_expression,             modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, arrow_function_expression,    modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, assignment_expression,        modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, await_expression,             modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, boolean_literal,              modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
-- ...and `null_literal`, added 2026-09-06 when `catch { return null; }` entered
-- the corpus with the propagation fixtures and `vocabulary_gap[audit]` named it
-- within one run — the second time in two iterations that gate has caught a kind
-- my own fixture introduced, which is the whole reason it counts rather than
-- reporting nothing.
node_kind(js, null_literal).
ignored(js, null_literal,                 modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, class_declaration,            modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, class_method,                 modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, conditional_expression,       modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, decorator,                    modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, for_of_statement,             modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, function_declaration,         modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, function_expression,          modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, if_statement,                 modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, logical_expression,           modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, member_expression,            modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, new_expression,               modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, numeric_literal,              modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, object_expression,            modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, object_method,                modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, object_property,              modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, optional_call_expression,     modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, optional_member_expression,   modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, parenthesized_expression,     modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, return_statement,             modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, sequence_expression,          modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, super,                        modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, tagged_template_expression,   modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, template_literal,             modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, this_expression,              modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, ts_string_keyword,            modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, tsas_expression,              modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, tsnon_null_expression,        modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, variable_declarator,          modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, yield_expression,             modules, a_not_a_module_construct).

-- ...AND THE SAME UNREACHABLE KIND, a third time. `import` is babel's `Import`
-- node and `plugins: ['typescript']` never emits it; the argument is identical
-- at all three layers and is written out rather than left to the default.
ignored(js, import, modules, a_babel_emits_no_import_callee).

-- ...AND THE TWO THE LAYER ACTUALLY RUNS ON. Neither was ever recorded, and
-- both are read by name in rules/js-modules.rofl: the specifier text is a
-- string literal (`site_source`), and every binding a module introduces is an
-- identifier (`r_named_binding`, `r_default_binding`, `r_namespace_binding`).
handled(js, string_literal, modules, r_import_decl_module_edge).
handled(js, identifier,     modules, r_named_binding).

-- ...and the one construct that IS a module edge and is not an import.
-- `require('./x')` parses as an ordinary call under `sourceType: 'module'`, so
-- the model can SEE it and says nothing. Whether CommonJS belongs in scope at
-- all is the owner's call - `bare` next to it already carries his
-- `out_of_scope` - and until he says, it is honest work rather than a waiver.
unknown_because(js, call_expression, modules, not_yet).


-- ===========================================================================
-- DECLARED HERE BECAUSE THIS PACK NOW JUDGES THEM, and `orphan_claim[audit]`
-- said so in one run: 31 kinds carried a modules verdict written above
-- while their `node_kind` rows live in the callgraph, dataflow and statements
-- packs, so in the world test/js-modules.test.ts builds the claims pointed at
-- cells that do not exist. Same correction as facts/js-callgraph.rofl made the
-- day before, and the SECOND time, which is what makes it a class rather than
-- a slip - see f_the_kind_vocabulary_is_scattered_across_the_packs_that_needed_it.
node_kind(js, array_expression).
node_kind(js, arrow_function_expression).
node_kind(js, await_expression).
node_kind(js, boolean_literal).
node_kind(js, class_declaration).
node_kind(js, class_method).
node_kind(js, conditional_expression).
node_kind(js, decorator).
node_kind(js, for_of_statement).
node_kind(js, function_expression).
node_kind(js, identifier).
node_kind(js, import).
node_kind(js, logical_expression).
node_kind(js, member_expression).
node_kind(js, new_expression).
node_kind(js, numeric_literal).
node_kind(js, object_expression).
node_kind(js, object_method).
node_kind(js, object_property).
node_kind(js, optional_call_expression).
node_kind(js, optional_member_expression).
node_kind(js, parenthesized_expression).
node_kind(js, return_statement).
node_kind(js, sequence_expression).
node_kind(js, string_literal).
node_kind(js, super).
node_kind(js, tagged_template_expression).
node_kind(js, template_literal).
node_kind(js, this_expression).
node_kind(js, tsas_expression).
node_kind(js, tsnon_null_expression).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE FOUR KINDS THE FRAME ADDED, 2026-09-06 (w_vocabulary_frame). The kind is
-- re-declared here because a file that makes a claim declares the kind — the
-- convention w_vocabulary_home exists to question, and the one in force.
node_kind(js, binary_expression).
node_kind(js, unary_expression).
node_kind(js, class_body).
node_kind(js, template_element).

ignored(js, binary_expression, modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, unary_expression, modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, class_body, modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, template_element, modules, a_not_a_module_construct).

-- TEN TYPE NODES, 2026-09-07 (w_type_surface). A type-only import still emits
-- an import_declaration, and THAT is what carries the module edge; the type
-- nodes under it contribute nothing of their own.
node_kind(js, tsfunction_type).
node_kind(js, tsnull_keyword).
node_kind(js, tsnumber_keyword).
node_kind(js, tsstring_keyword).
node_kind(js, tstype_alias_declaration).
node_kind(js, tstype_annotation).
node_kind(js, tstype_parameter_instantiation).
node_kind(js, tstype_query).
node_kind(js, tstype_reference).
node_kind(js, tsunion_type).

ignored(js, tsfunction_type,                modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, tsnull_keyword,                 modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, tsnumber_keyword,               modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, tsstring_keyword,               modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, tstype_alias_declaration,       modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, tstype_annotation,              modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, tstype_parameter_instantiation, modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, tstype_query,                   modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, tstype_reference,               modules, a_not_a_module_construct).
ignored(js, tsunion_type,                   modules, a_not_a_module_construct).

-- DECLARED HERE AND ABSENT FROM THE CORPUS, with the reason written down;
-- `kind_unexercised[audit]` demands one, and a row belongs where its kind is
-- declared.
kind_absent_ok(export_all_declaration, a_no_corpus_site).
kind_absent_ok(import, a_babel_emits_no_import_callee).
kind_absent_ok(import_default_specifier, a_no_corpus_site).
kind_absent_ok(import_namespace_specifier, a_no_corpus_site).

-- AN EXCUSE IS COPIED WHEREVER ITS KIND IS DECLARED, for the same reason the
-- `node_kind` rows are: a world loading this pack and not the other declares
-- the kind and would carry no reason for its absence.
-- `kind_unexercised[audit]` named the missing copies within one run.
kind_absent_ok(decorator, a_no_corpus_site).
kind_absent_ok(tagged_template_expression, a_no_corpus_site).
kind_absent_ok(parenthesized_expression, a_babel_emits_no_parenthesized_node).
